+This supersedes an earlier decision in this file for HDFS on Chameleon bare metal.
+That decision rested on two arguments — S3 egress cost and data locality — both of
+which are on-prem arguments that do not survive the move to EC2: in-region S3<->EC2
+transfer is free, and a large instance's network bandwidth is in the same league as
+its local NVMe.
 
-A2, A3, and A4 all fall out of this decision — make it before writing that code.
+| Option | At 16 nodes | |
+|---|---|---|
+| NFS from one node | single server saturates; measures the NFS box, not the listener | disqualifying |
+| Replicate 1TB per node | perfect locality, keeps `file://`, but 16 stagings and an unrealistic deployment | rejected |
+| HDFS co-located on compute | bandwidth scales with node count, but *storage changes along the measured axis* | fallback only |
+| **S3 read directly** | storage layer identical at every node count; durable across teardown | **chosen** |
+
+The methodological fact underneath all of this: **the slower the storage, the smaller
+the measured listener overhead appears**, because an I/O-bound scan hides listener CPU
+in I/O wait. That does not disqualify S3 — it determines what has to be said about the
+result (§4.2, §4.3).
+
+Setup, on EC2:
+
+- **`hadoop-aws` 3.3.4**, matching `hadoop.version` in the Spark fork's `pom.xml`,
+  with the AWS SDK bundle version that POM pins. Mismatch here fails at runtime as
+  `NoSuchMethodError`, not at build time.
+- **`fs.s3a.experimental.input.fadvise=random`** — required for Parquet. The sequential
+  default over-reads badly on columnar range requests.
+- **Raise `fs.s3a.connection.maximum`** (default 96) and `fs.s3a.threads.max` together;
+  at 16 nodes the default exhausts and surfaces as a hang, not an error.
+- **IAM instance profile**, not static keys — EC2 has an instance metadata service, so
+  nothing needs distributing to 16 nodes.
+- **S3 gateway VPC endpoint**: traffic stays inside the VPC, no NAT charges, no internet
+  path.
+- Spread keys across prefixes to stay under the per-prefix request ceiling.
+
+Security posture comes largely free from the network design: put workers in a
+**private subnet with no public IPs** and reach them via SSM rather than inbound SSH.
+This matters because an exposed Spark standalone master on 7077 is unauthenticated
+remote code execution and is actively scanned for. Do **not** mitigate with
+`spark.authenticate` / `spark.network.crypto.enabled` — both add per-connection and
+per-byte overhead that would contaminate the very deltas being measured, unevenly
+across node counts as shuffle volume grows.
+
+### 4.1 Why S3 over HDFS on EC2
+
+- **Teardown economics.** On instance storage the data dies with the instances, and
+  16 nodes idling is roughly $44/hr (~$1,000/day) to keep it alive. So you terminate
+  between sessions and re-stage 1TB at every bring-up. In S3 the data persists at
+  ~$23/month and the cluster is disposable.
+- **It decouples storage from the measured axis.** With HDFS co-located on compute,
+  going 1 -> 4 -> 8 -> 16 changes the storage system *at the same time* as the
+  compute. With S3 the storage layer is byte-identical at every node count and only
+  parallelism varies.
+- **It closes the prototype gap more completely.** R2D14 / R3W1 / R3D1 are complaints
+  about prototype-vs-evaluation mismatch. The prototype is Spark/Databricks + Unity
+  Catalog, which reads object storage. HDFS leaves a residual version of the same
+  objection.
+- **One staging serves all three experiments.** Placement explicitly wants an
+  S3 + local-cache hierarchy (`REVISION_EXPERIMENTS.md` §3.2), so §6 gets one storage
+  story instead of two.
+- **Reproducible for artifact evaluation.** A bucket is something a reviewer can point
+  at; a torn-down HDFS cluster is not.
+- Less setup: no namenode, no formatting, no `dfs.datanode.data.dir` on the right
+  disks, no re-balancing at each cluster size.
+
+### 4.2 The one thing HDFS wins, and why it is not disqualifying
+
+HDFS co-scaled with compute keeps the cluster **compute-bound at every node count**,
+because aggregate read bandwidth grows with N. With S3 the endpoint bandwidth is fixed
+while demand grows, so at 16 nodes the scan may become I/O-bound where at 1 node it
+never was — and listener CPU then hides in I/O wait.
+
+That is a real effect, but it is **in scope rather than a confound**: blob storage plus
+elastic compute is the common deployment, so measuring in that regime is measuring the
+deployment people actually run. The requirement it creates is disclosure, not
+avoidance — see §4.3.
+
+Other S3 quirks are manageable. GET request cost is negligible at this volume.
+`fs.s3a.experimental.input.fadvise=random` matters for read amplification but does not
+corrupt the row-group accumulator, which records the row groups the *filter selected*,
+not bytes fetched from storage. Also needed: `hadoop-aws` at 3.3.4 with a version-matched
+AWS SDK bundle, a raised `fs.s3a.connection.maximum` (default 96 is low for 16 nodes),
+and keys spread across prefixes to stay under per-prefix request limits.
+
+### 4.3 Pre-registered hypothesis: overhead decreases as the cluster grows
+
+Stated **before the runs**, following the "predict the direction before running"
+convention in `REVISION_EXPERIMENTS.md` §2. This matters more than usual because the
+predicted result *flatters* VDI; a pre-registered prediction plus absolute numbers is
+what makes it defensible rather than post-hoc.
+
+**Hypothesis:** relative listener overhead falls as node count rises. Answering R2D16
+("does the Figure 13/14 trend continue?") with "it continues, and overhead becomes less
+significant at scale" is the expected outcome.
+
+**Two mechanisms produce that same downward curve, and they mean different things:**
+
+| | Mechanism | What it says |
+|---|---|---|
+| **Amortization** | listener cost is largely per-query and driver-side — plan walk, accumulator merge, log write — so a roughly fixed cost is a smaller fraction of a larger total | a genuine scaling property **of the VDI design**; ours to claim |
+| **Masking** | listener CPU disappears into I/O wait because the cluster is storage-bound | a property **of S3**; not ours to claim |
+
+Both must be addressed in the §6 discussion when describing why overhead decreases.
+They are separable with data already being collected:
+
+1. **Report absolute overhead (ms) alongside relative (%).** The cheapest and most
+   decisive step. Flat absolute per-query listener cost with a falling percentage is
+   amortization, unambiguously. A reviewer shown only percentages cannot tell the two
+   apart and will reasonably suspect masking.
+2. **`executorCpuTime` vs `executorRunTime`** per stage, from Spark's own task metrics.
+   A declining CPU-to-runtime ratio as N grows means the cluster is becoming I/O-bound
+   and masking is in play; a flat ratio means it is not.
+3. **The variant ladder** (`register` -> `bare` -> `nowrite` -> `cols` -> `rowgroup`)
+   already decomposes where listener cost sits. If the write-bearing variants converge
+   toward `nowrite` at 16 nodes, the log write specifically is being masked.
+
+**Honest framing for §6:** state that overhead declines with scale, give the absolute
+figures showing listener cost is roughly constant per query, and say explicitly that
+part of the relative decline reflects the cluster becoming storage-bound at high node
+counts — which is itself representative of production object-store deployments. Do not
+claim the decline is purely an amortization property unless (2) shows a flat CPU ratio.
+
+### 4.4 If HDFS is needed after all
+
+Hydrate HDFS from S3 at cluster start as a run-time cache layer — roughly 15 minutes of
+bring-up at 10 Gbps aggregate, paid per session rather than per experiment. Trigger is
+the §6 check, not preference. Settings if used: Hadoop 3.3.4 (matching `hadoop.version`
+in the Spark fork's `pom.xml`), `dfs.replication=2` uniform across node counts
+(at N=1 the block stays under-replicated — `fsck` warns, reads unaffected; record it
+rather than special-casing RF=1), and `dfs.datanode.data.dir` pointed at the instance
+NVMe rather than the root volume.
+
+**S3 is the durable copy either way**, since it is the only thing that survives cluster
+teardown.
 
